Isaac's '''fire rate''' is primarily tied to the hidden Tears attribute, which decreases the base delay between fired tears. Items and such that directly modify fire rate also exist, but are rarer than ones that affect the Tears attribute. The game uses an icon depicting three arrows in flight to represent both fire rate and the Tears attribute; In the Found HUD, the arrows denote either {{dlc|nr}} Isaac's tear delay {{dlc|r}} or his fire rate in tears per second.

== Fire Rate Calculation ==
The formula for calculating fire rate is:
<math>
\text{Fire Rate} = ({\frac{30}{\text{Tear Delay} + 1} + {\text{S}}) * {\text{P}}}
</math>

Where tear delay is calculated using the Tears stat {{dlc|anr}}and tear delay modifiers, S is the sum of all direct additions and subtractions to fire rate, and P is the product of all direct fire rate multipliers.

As the vast majority of items that affect fire rate do so by adding or subtracting Tears, tear delay is the most commonly relevant part of the formula. Depending on how high Isaac's Tears stat is, one of four different formulas is used to calculate tear delay:

<math>
\text{Tear Delay} =
\begin{cases}
  5                                                    & \text{if } T > T_{max}\\
  16 - 6 \times \sqrt{T \times 1.3 {+} 1}              & \text{if } T \geq 0 \text{ and } T \leq T_{max}\\
  16 - 6 \times \sqrt{T \times 1.3 {+} 1} - 6 \times T & \text{if } T < 0 \text{ and } T > -0.77\\
  16 - 6 \times T                                      & \text{if } T \leq -0.77
\end{cases}
</math>

* <math>T</math> is the Tears stat.
* <math>T_{max}</math> is the Tears cap, defined as the number that brings you to 5 tear delay. Increasing the tears stat beyond this does nothing directly, but it can counteract potential tears down. If Isaac has no direct fire rate modifiers, he will have a fire rate of 5 tears per second at <math>T_{max}</math> Tears.
* {{dlc|nr}} Tear Delay is rounded down to the nearest integer. <math>T_{max}</math> is just under 1.37, meaning two items with +0.7 tears is enough to get your tear delay to under 6 and therefore to 5, assuming you start at 0.
* {{dlc|r}} Tear Delay is no longer rounded down. <math>T_{max}</math> is just under 1.82 (1.816239316).

Tear delay is '''the additional delay between fired tears beyond 1/30 seconds''', in units of 1/30 seconds. For example, 7 tear delay means 8/30 seconds between tears, or 30/8 = 3.75 fire rate.

{{dlc|nr}} Items can directly reduce tear delay.
* The minimum tear delay is 1, corresponding to 15 fire rate.
* Tear delay reduction has accelerating returns. Each -1 tear delay is more effective the more tear delay reduction (and the higher tears stat) you have.

{{dlc|r}} Almost all direct tear delay reductions were replaced with fire rate increases.
* Generally, each -1 tear delay is replaced with a +0.5 fire rate increase.
* The maximum fire rate is 120.

Items that directly affect fire rate include:
* {{i|Pisces}}, which gives +0.5 fire rate.
* {{i|Soy Milk}}, which gives a x5.5 fire rate multiplier.
* {{i|The Inner Eye}}, which gives a x0.51 fire rate multiplier.
There are many other rules and exceptions.{{explain}}

The tears stat has accelerating returns. Each increase of some +X tears provides about the same percentage bonus to fire rate (and therefore damage per second) until you reach the cap. For example, if you get +0.35 tears (a tears up pill) five times, each gives 12-13% fire rate increase, with the first one giving +0.35 fire rate and the last one +0.55 fire rate.

At or below -0.77 Tears, changes to the Tears stat affect fire rate much less. This is especially relevant for {{c|Keeper}}, who starts with -1.9 Tears, and {{c|Tainted Keeper}}, who starts with -2.2 Tears.

Fire rate on the other hand has linear returns. Each +1 fire rate increases shots per second by the same amount. Fire rate increases are applied before fire rate multipliers, so with a multiplier of 2, a +1 fire rate addition will increase fire rate by 2.

